Apply to src/lib/auth.ts in the ScoreHUB repo.

WHY: a cookie set with SameSite=Lax is not sent on cross-site requests. As long
as the app and API shared an origin that was the right, safest setting. Once the
front end lives on a different origin (GitHub Pages, Netlify, a custom domain)
the browser silently drops the session cookie on every API call and the static
site behaves as though nobody is signed in — no error, just 401s.

SameSite=None re-enables it, and the browser only honours that together with
Secure, which means the API must be served over HTTPS. This is a real widening:
the cookie now travels on cross-site requests, so CORS (see middleware.ts) is
what keeps other sites from using it. Do not set None without that middleware
restricting Access-Control-Allow-Origin to origins you control.

--- a/src/lib/auth.ts
+++ b/src/lib/auth.ts
@@
   const store = await cookies();
   store.set(COOKIE_NAME, token, {
     httpOnly: true,
-    sameSite: 'lax',
-    secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production',
+    // Cross-site: the static front end is on a different origin to this API.
+    // 'none' requires 'secure', so the API has to be on HTTPS.
+    sameSite: CROSS_SITE ? 'none' : 'lax',
+    secure: CROSS_SITE || process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production',
     path: '/',
     maxAge: TTL,
   });

Add near the top of the same file, beside the other constants:

+/**
+ * Set CROSS_SITE_COOKIE=true when the front end is served from a different
+ * origin to this API. Leave it unset for the all-in-one Next.js deployment,
+ * which keeps the stricter SameSite=Lax.
+ */
+const CROSS_SITE = process.env.CROSS_SITE_COOKIE === 'true';

Then set on the API host:

  CROSS_SITE_COOKIE=true
  ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://vchandanshive34.github.io

And on the realtime host, so the socket accepts the same origin:

  ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://vchandanshive34.github.io
